course: dictionary
lesson: 3553
title: Fellow: a man, a mate, or one of a pair
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The word «fellow» can mean different things, so context tells you which meaning to use. Look at the words next to it and ask: what would fit here?
explain: In «fellow students» and «fellow pupils», fellow means others in the same group as you, like classmates. In «a funny old fellow», it means a man or boy, in a friendly, informal, old-fashioned way, like saying a chap or a guy.
explain: In «a sock without its fellow», fellow means the other one of a pair. Try your meaning in the sentence: if «the other one of a pair» still makes sense, you have chosen well.
fact: Fellow can be a noun (a fellow) or an adjective (fellow, before a noun).
fact: As an adjective, fellow means in the same group or situation as you, as in «fellow citizens» or «fellow travellers».
fact: As a noun, «a fellow» can mean a man or boy. This use is informal and a bit old-fashioned.
fact: «Fellow» can also mean one of a pair, as in «a sock without its fellow», or a member of a society.
exercise: «They were fellow students at the same school.» What does fellow mean here? | A sock with no pair | A man who is very rich | *Others in the same group as you | Old and tired
exercise: In an old story, Toad says «What's become of them, the dear fellows?» What does fellows mean? | *Men or boys, in an old-fashioned way | Pairs of socks | Sheep, because they are woolly | Teachers at a school
exercise: «I found one glove, but not its fellow.» What does fellow mean? | A man who lives next door | A member of a club | *The other one of a pair | A fellow pupil
test: Which meaning fits «fellow travellers»? | People who fly planes for a living | Rich old men who own ships | *People on the same journey as you | Rivals racing against you
test: In «a funny old fellow», what does fellow mean? | *A man, said in an informal, old-fashioned way | A school in the country | A rule that everyone must follow | A pair of shoes
exam: Which sentence uses fellow to mean one of a pair? | «The fellow at the door waved.» | «My fellow pupils are kind.» | *«I found one sock, but not its fellow.» | «He is a nice fellow.»
